window.CHEAT_SHEET_SECTIONS=[{"id": "start", "title": "1. Start here: scope and priorities", "html": "<p>Year 12 Enterprise Computing · Data Science and Visualisation · revision edition 24 November 2026. Use this to learn and practise before the assessment. The notification does not permit personal notes or prepared formulas during the actual task.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Assessment</th><th>What to prepare</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>80 minutes · 40 marks</td><td>Section A: 24 marks, unseen spreadsheet dataset, formulas, filtering and chart interpretation. Section B: 16 marks, supplied dashboard, recommendation, limitation and system change.</td></tr><tr><td>Highest practical priority</td><td>Combined filters; AVERAGEIF; exact VLOOKUP; IF including equality; accurate formula/result/unit reporting. These are directly reflected in the teacher guidance and school practice.</td></tr><tr><td>Highest written priority</td><td>Compare specific figures; use rates fairly; recommend an action with evidence; explain how a limitation could affect it; explain a proposed system change.</td></tr><tr><td>Full revision scope</td><td>Spreadsheet Modules 1–5 cover Lessons 1–16. Booklet Lessons 14–19 cover UX, creating and presenting visualisations, security, errors/bias and blockchain.</td></tr><tr><td>How to read this sheet</td><td>Sections 3–5: learn the core operations first. Section 6: other formulas explicitly named in the modules. Section 7: useful supporting functions, not a guaranteed exam list. All question predictions are inferences.</td></tr><tr><td>Actual exam workflow</td><td>Use the dataset and Excel version/method your teacher authorises. Submit requested formulas, results and written answers in Canvas before the session ends. An Excel whitelist does not authorise personal notes or AI.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "data", "title": "2. One consistent dataset for the worked formulas", "html": "<p>The examples below use the fictional 12-ticket CSV already in your study app. <strong>Its column letters are examples, not the layout of your unseen test.</strong> Read the actual headers before adapting a formula.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>TicketID</td></tr><tr><td><code>B</code></td><td>Region</td></tr><tr><td><code>C</code></td><td>Category</td></tr><tr><td><code>D</code></td><td>Urgency</td></tr><tr><td><code>E</code></td><td>ResolutionMinutes</td></tr><tr><td><code>F</code></td><td>TargetMinutes</td></tr><tr><td><code>G</code></td><td>Cost</td></tr><tr><td><code>H</code></td><td>Status: add the IF formula in H2 and fill through H13.</td></tr><tr><td><code>J2</code></td><td>Separate common target input: 25 minutes. Use only when the question specifies this rule.</td></tr><tr><td><code>L2</code></td><td>Separate lookup input: type T208.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Headers are row 1; records are rows 2–13. Each row is one ticket. F holds that ticket's individual target. J2 holds a different, shared target for a separate what-if example. Do not substitute one rule for the other.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Expected value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All tickets / total cost</td><td>12 tickets / $748</td></tr><tr><td>North / South mean resolution</td><td>28.20 / 37.75 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>North AND Network</td><td>T201, T206, T209 · 3 tickets</td></tr><tr><td>T208 resolution / number above 30 minutes</td><td>60 minutes / 5 tickets</td></tr><tr><td>Met individual target / over individual target</td><td>6 / 6 tickets; 50% each</td></tr><tr><td>Mean / median / sample standard deviation</td><td>31.17 / 26.50 / 13.93 minutes (displayed to 2 decimal places)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "basics", "title": "3. Formula grammar, operators and cell references", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Meaning / example</th><th>Avoid this mistake</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Formula versus function</td><td><code>=E2+F2</code> is a formula. <code>=SUM(E2:E13)</code> uses a built-in function.</td><td>Every function formula starts with =. A result typed by hand will not recalculate.</td></tr><tr><td>Range</td><td><code>E2:E13</code> includes every cell from E2 to E13, inclusive.</td><td>Do not include a total row as well as the detail being totalled.</td></tr><tr><td>Arguments</td><td><code>=AVERAGEIF(B2:B13,&quot;North&quot;,E2:E13)</code> has three arguments, separated by commas.</td><td>Commas are the examples' separator. If the approved Excel locale expects semicolons, use its accepted separator.</td></tr><tr><td>Text and cell references</td><td><code>&quot;North&quot;</code> is text; <code>L2</code> is the value in a cell.</td><td>Use straight quotes around text, not around a cell reference. <code>&quot;L2&quot;</code> searches for the literal letters L2.</td></tr><tr><td>Arithmetic</td><td><code>+</code> add; <code>-</code> subtract; <code>*</code> multiply; <code>/</code> divide; <code>^</code> exponent.</td><td>Excel multiplication is *, not ×; division is /, not ÷. Use brackets to make order clear.</td></tr><tr><td>Order</td><td><code>=(20+10)/2</code> = 15; <code>=20+10/2</code> = 25.</td><td>Multiplication/division occur before addition/subtraction unless parentheses change the grouping.</td></tr><tr><td>Comparison</td><td><code>=</code> equal; <code>&lt;&gt;</code> not equal; <code>&gt;</code> above; <code>&gt;=</code> at least; <code>&lt;</code> below; <code>&lt;=</code> at most.</td><td>At most / no more than / within the target usually includes equality. Over / more than excludes equality.</td></tr><tr><td>Relative reference</td><td><code>E2</code> becomes <code>E3</code> when copied down one row.</td><td>Useful when each record should use its own value.</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute reference</td><td><code>$J$2</code> stays <code>$J$2</code> when copied across or down.</td><td>Use this for one common input or a fixed lookup table boundary.</td></tr><tr><td>Mixed reference</td><td><code>$B2</code> locks column B; <code>B$2</code> locks row 2.</td><td>The $ fixes the part immediately following it. It does not make a number into currency.</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-sheet reference</td><td><code>=ClassA!D2</code> or <code>=&#x27;Ticket Data&#x27;!E2</code></td><td>Use single quotes around sheet names containing spaces. Linking is different from pasting a static value.</td></tr><tr><td>Copy down / fill handle</td><td>Enter the formula in the first record row; fill through the last record; inspect a middle and final formula.</td><td>Lock only references that should remain fixed. Check the range includes new records when data grows.</td></tr><tr><td>Formatting versus value</td><td>Displaying 2 decimal places changes appearance. <code>ROUND</code> changes the calculated result.</td><td>Calculate with full precision where practical; round final reporting as instructed.</td></tr><tr><td>Percent format</td><td><code>=6/12</code> gives 0.5, displayed as 50% with Percentage format.</td><td>Do not also multiply by 100 before applying Percentage format.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "core", "title": "4. Core formula table: learn these first", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Formula / when to use it</th><th>Worked Excel example</th><th>Result / reading</th><th>Common mistake</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SUM</strong><br>Total an amount.</td><td><code>=SUM(G2:G13)</code></td><td>$748; adds the Cost values.</td><td>Adding IDs or averaging when asked for a total.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AVERAGE</strong><br>Mean of numeric values.</td><td><code>=AVERAGE(E2:E13)</code></td><td>31.1666… minutes; display 31.17.</td><td>A mean is not the middle value. Blank/text cells in a referenced range are ignored, while a numeric zero is included.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MIN / MAX</strong><br>Smallest / largest numeric value.</td><td><code>=MIN(E2:E13)</code></td><td>15 minutes. <code>=MAX(E2:E13)</code> returns 60.</td><td>A minimum or maximum does not identify the record's ID by itself.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>COUNT</strong><br>Count numeric cells.</td><td><code>=COUNT(E2:E13)</code></td><td>12 numeric resolution times.</td><td>COUNT does not count text IDs. A missing time means it may count fewer tickets than exist.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>COUNTA</strong><br>Count non-empty cells.</td><td><code>=COUNTA(A2:A13)</code></td><td>12 ticket IDs.</td><td>Choose a complete ID column, exclude the header and avoid formula-produced empty strings if using it as a record count.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>COUNTIF</strong><br>Count cells matching one condition.</td><td><code>=COUNTIF(B2:B13,&quot;North&quot;)</code></td><td>5 North tickets.</td><td>Counts matching records, not their total cost.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>COUNTIF · numeric rule</strong><br>Count measurements above a boundary.</td><td><code>=COUNTIF(E2:E13,&quot;&gt;30&quot;)</code></td><td>5 tickets. Exactly 30 would be excluded.</td><td>Comparisons used as criteria need quotes. For an input in J2 use <code>&quot;&gt;&quot;&amp;$J$2</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SUMIF</strong><br>Add amounts for a matching group.</td><td><code>=SUMIF(B2:B13,&quot;North&quot;,G2:G13)</code></td><td>$282. Syntax: <code>=SUMIF(check_range,criterion,sum_range)</code>.</td><td>Check the labels in B, but sum costs in G. Keep corresponding rows aligned.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AVERAGEIF · highest priority</strong><br>Mean for a matching group.</td><td><code>=AVERAGEIF(B2:B13,&quot;North&quot;,E2:E13)</code></td><td>28.20 minutes. Syntax: <code>=AVERAGEIF(check_range,criterion,average_range)</code>.</td><td>The first range has group labels; the last has measurements. For South, change only the criterion. No matching numeric values can produce #DIV/0!.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IF · individual target</strong><br>Choose between two outputs using a rule.</td><td><code>=IF(E2&lt;=F2,&quot;Met&quot;,&quot;Over target&quot;)</code></td><td>In H2: T201 is Met because 18 ≤ 20. Fill through H13.</td><td>Exactly equal must return Met here. Numeric inputs/cell references are not quoted; output labels are quoted.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IF · common target</strong><br>Use one fixed target for every record.</td><td><code>=IF(E2&lt;=$J$2,&quot;Met&quot;,&quot;Over target&quot;)</code></td><td>With J2 = 25, a 25-minute ticket is Met. Change J2 to 24 and it becomes Over target.</td><td>Do not lock E2: each row should read its own resolution time. Record the first result before changing the input.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>VLOOKUP · highest priority</strong><br>Retrieve a field belonging to an exact ID.</td><td><code>=VLOOKUP(&quot;T208&quot;,$A$2:$G$13,5,FALSE)</code></td><td>60 minutes. Syntax: <code>=VLOOKUP(value,table,return_column,FALSE)</code>.</td><td>ID must be in the selected table's first column. Count the return column within that table. FALSE means exact; omitting it allows approximate matching.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Build AVERAGEIF in words:</strong> where are the labels? → which label? → which same-row numbers? <strong>Build VLOOKUP in words:</strong> which ID? → which table starts with IDs? → which relative column? → exact match. <strong>Build IF in words:</strong> test → output if true → output if false.</p><p>Ordinary SUM, AVERAGE, COUNTIF, SUMIF and AVERAGEIF do not become visible-row-only calculations when you filter a sheet. They still evaluate their specified ranges and criteria. Use SUBTOTAL for a visible-only summary, or state all required conditions explicitly in a conditional function. Follow the question's required function.</p>"}, {"id": "multi", "title": "5. Multiple conditions and applying rules", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning / expected result</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>COUNTIFS</strong><br>Every condition must match.</td><td><code>=COUNTIFS(B2:B13,&quot;North&quot;,C2:C13,&quot;Network&quot;)</code></td><td>3 tickets.</td><td>Use same-sized, aligned criteria ranges. These conditions combine as AND.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SUMIFS</strong><br>Sum matching rows.</td><td><code>=SUMIFS(G2:G13,B2:B13,&quot;North&quot;,C2:C13,&quot;Network&quot;)</code></td><td>$132 for North Network tickets.</td><td>SUMIFS puts the sum range first. SUMIF puts it last.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AVERAGEIFS</strong><br>Mean of matching rows.</td><td><code>=AVERAGEIFS(E2:E13,B2:B13,&quot;North&quot;,C2:C13,&quot;Network&quot;)</code></td><td>22 minutes.</td><td>AVERAGEIFS puts the average range first. AVERAGEIF puts it last.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AND</strong><br>All tests must be true.</td><td><code>=AND(B2=&quot;North&quot;,E2&lt;=F2)</code></td><td>TRUE for T201.</td><td>AND itself returns TRUE/FALSE. Combine it with IF to return labels.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>OR</strong><br>At least one test must be true.</td><td><code>=OR(D2=&quot;High&quot;,E2&gt;F2)</code></td><td>TRUE for T201 because urgency is High.</td><td>OR includes a record satisfying either or both tests. Do not double-count an overlap when adding separate counts.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IF + AND</strong><br>Classify with two required conditions.</td><td><code>=IF(AND(B2=&quot;North&quot;,E2&lt;=F2),&quot;North met&quot;,&quot;Other&quot;)</code></td><td>North met for T201.</td><td>A North ticket that exceeded its target must return Other.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IF + OR</strong><br>Flag either of two issues.</td><td><code>=IF(OR(D2=&quot;High&quot;,E2&gt;F2),&quot;Review&quot;,&quot;Routine&quot;)</code></td><td>Review for T201.</td><td>This is a fictional review rule. Apply the exact conditions stated in the actual question.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nested IF</strong><br>Three bands using two tests.</td><td><code>=IF(E2&lt;=20,&quot;Up to 20&quot;,IF(E2&lt;=40,&quot;Over 20 to 40&quot;,&quot;Over 40&quot;))</code></td><td>Up to 20 for T201.</td><td>Test bands in a sensible order. Test values below, equal to and above each boundary; avoid gaps and overlapping decisions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These multi-condition formulas are useful supporting tools, not a guarantee that the exam asks for each one. For filters, selections of North OR South within the Region column usually combine as OR; Region = North together with Category = Network combines as AND across columns.</p>"}, {"id": "moduleformulas", "title": "6. Other formulas named in your spreadsheet modules", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Formula / when</th><th>Worked example</th><th>Meaning / result</th><th>Watch for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>MEDIAN</strong><br>Middle after ordering the numeric values.</td><td><code>=MEDIAN(E2:E13)</code></td><td>26.50 minutes; for an even count, average the two middle values.</td><td>Less sensitive to extreme values than the mean. It does not prove an outlier is an error.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MODE / MODE.SNGL</strong><br>Most frequent numeric value.</td><td><code>=MODE.SNGL(F2:F13)</code></td><td>20 minutes is the most frequent target. Your course uses the name MODE.</td><td>For the E times, all values are unique, so MODE.SNGL returns #N/A. No mode is a valid finding; do not invent one.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>STDEV / STDEV.S</strong><br>Sample standard deviation: spread around the mean.</td><td><code>=STDEV.S(E2:E13)</code></td><td>13.93 minutes. The course's <code>=STDEV(E2:E13)</code> is the older sample function.</td><td>Larger means more spread for the same measure/scale. Use STDEV.P only if treating the data as the whole population; it gives 13.34 here.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>HLOOKUP</strong><br>Search horizontally across a table's first row.</td><td><code>=HLOOKUP(&quot;South&quot;,$J$5:$L$6,2,FALSE)</code></td><td>Finds South in the top row and returns row 2 of this separate horizontal table.</td><td>For this example only: J5:L5 = North, South, West; J6:L6 = 28.20, 37.75, 27.3333…. Result = 37.75. Do not apply it to the vertical ticket table.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MATCH</strong><br>Find an item's relative position.</td><td><code>=MATCH(&quot;T208&quot;,A2:A13,0)</code></td><td>8: the eighth item in A2:A13, not worksheet row 8.</td><td>Use 0 for exact matching; position is not the desired time or cost.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>INDEX</strong><br>Return the item at a position in a range.</td><td><code>=INDEX(E2:E13,8)</code></td><td>60 minutes.</td><td>Positions are counted within the selected range.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>INDEX + MATCH</strong><br>Flexible exact lookup, including to the left.</td><td><code>=INDEX(E2:E13,MATCH(L2,A2:A13,0))</code></td><td>60 minutes when L2 contains T208.</td><td>Return and lookup ranges must describe the same ordered records.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cross-sheet link</strong><br>Read another sheet's result.</td><td><code>=ClassA!D2</code></td><td>Returns D2 from the ClassA sheet.</td><td>A sheet reference uses !, not a comma. A link updates with the source; pasted values do not.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cross-sheet summary</strong><br>Summarise values on another sheet.</td><td><code>=AVERAGE(ClassA!D2:D5)</code></td><td>Mean of the four numeric totals on ClassA.</td><td>Use the question's actual sheet name and range; exclude any included subtotal.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "support", "title": "7. Supporting functions: useful extras, lower revision priority", "html": "<p>These help solve and check tasks. They are included for completeness, not because every one is named in the notification or must be memorised before the core functions.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Function / purpose</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ROUND</strong><br>Round a calculated result.</td><td><code>=ROUND(AVERAGE(E2:E13),2)</code></td><td>31.17.</td><td>Keep full precision in intermediate calculations where possible. Formatting alone does not alter the stored result.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SUBTOTAL · visible sum</strong><br>Sum a filtered vertical list.</td><td><code>=SUBTOTAL(109,G2:G13)</code></td><td>Totals visible costs. With North AND Network filtered, $132.</td><td>109 excludes filtered-out and manually hidden rows. Use a formula cell that stays visible.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SUBTOTAL · visible count</strong><br>Count visible non-empty ID cells.</td><td><code>=SUBTOTAL(103,A2:A13)</code></td><td>With North AND Network filtered, 3.</td><td>Counts non-empty IDs, not distinct IDs. Headers must be excluded.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SUBTOTAL · visible mean</strong><br>Average visible numeric measurements.</td><td><code>=SUBTOTAL(101,E2:E13)</code></td><td>With North AND Network filtered, 22 minutes.</td><td>101 = AVERAGE; 103 = COUNTA; 109 = SUM. Clearing filters changes the relevant visible population.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IFERROR</strong><br>Show a useful message for an error.</td><td><code>=IFERROR(VLOOKUP(L2,$A$2:$G$13,5,FALSE),&quot;Check ID&quot;)</code></td><td>60 for a valid T208 input; a message if an error occurs.</td><td>First fix the cause. Replacing every error with 0 can misrepresent missing data as a genuine zero.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>XLOOKUP</strong><br>Modern alternative with separate search and return ranges.</td><td><code>=XLOOKUP(L2,A2:A13,E2:E13,&quot;Check ID&quot;)</code></td><td>60 when L2 contains T208. Exact match is the default.</td><td>Use VLOOKUP if the question asks for VLOOKUP. Confirm availability in the approved Excel version.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TRIM</strong><br>Remove excess ordinary spaces.</td><td><code>=TRIM(B2)</code></td><td>North, or a cleaned version of a spaced label.</td><td>Not every unusual space character is removed. Clean source labels consistently before grouping.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CLEAN</strong><br>Remove certain non-printing characters.</td><td><code>=CLEAN(B2)</code></td><td>A cleaned text value.</td><td>Does not remove every possible Unicode character or verify that the value is true.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UPPER / LOWER</strong><br>Standardise case.</td><td><code>=UPPER(B2)</code></td><td>NORTH. <code>=LOWER(B2)</code> returns north.</td><td>Case standardisation cannot fix a genuinely wrong category.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LEFT / RIGHT</strong><br>Extract text from an end.</td><td><code>=LEFT(A2,1)</code></td><td>T. <code>=RIGHT(A2,3)</code> returns the text 201.</td><td>Extracted digits are text until converted when a calculation needs a number. Preserve leading zeroes in IDs.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LEN</strong><br>Count characters.</td><td><code>=LEN(A2)</code></td><td>4 characters in T201.</td><td>Useful for checking ID lengths; length alone does not validate an ID.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Join text / CONCATENATE</strong><br>Build a readable label.</td><td><code>=B2&amp;&quot; - &quot;&amp;A2</code></td><td>North - T201. Equivalent: <code>=CONCATENATE(B2,&quot; - &quot;,A2)</code>.</td><td>Supply spaces/punctuation explicitly. Keep numeric data numeric for analysis.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>VALUE</strong><br>Convert a recognised number string to a number.</td><td><code>=VALUE(&quot;25&quot;)</code></td><td>25 as a numeric value.</td><td>Do not convert identifier codes merely because they contain digits. Check units, locale and leading zeroes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "math", "title": "8. Calculations for interpreting enterprise results", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Calculation</th><th>Formula / example</th><th>Interpretation / trap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Absolute difference</td><td><code>=37.75-28.20</code> → 9.55 minutes.</td><td>State direction and units: South is 9.55 minutes longer on average in the sample.</td></tr><tr><td>Proportion / rate</td><td>Affected records ÷ relevant total. <code>=COUNTIF(H2:H13,&quot;Met&quot;)/COUNTA(A2:A13)</code> → 0.5; format as 50%.</td><td>Requires all H statuses filled correctly and one non-empty ID per ticket. Use the same population and period in numerator and denominator.</td></tr><tr><td>Percentage change</td><td>(New − Old) ÷ Old. From 20 to 30: <code>=(30-20)/20</code> → 50% when formatted as a percentage.</td><td>The old value is the denominator. A zero old value makes this calculation undefined.</td></tr><tr><td>Percentage-point difference</td><td>New percentage − old percentage. 30% − 20% = 10 percentage points.</td><td>The same move is a 50% relative increase. Do not call it merely a 10% increase.</td></tr><tr><td>Weighted / pooled mean</td><td>Sum of (group count × group mean) ÷ total count.</td><td>Use only comparable measures/periods. A simple average of group means can mislead when group sizes differ.</td></tr><tr><td>Overall rate</td><td>Sum of affected counts ÷ sum of relevant totals.</td><td>Do not average regional percentages without accounting for their different denominators.</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue / cost / profit</td><td>Revenue = quantity × price. Profit = revenue − relevant costs.</td><td>A larger revenue total does not prove a larger profit. Missing cost data can limit the recommendation.</td></tr><tr><td>Profit margin / markup</td><td>Margin = profit ÷ revenue. Markup = profit ÷ cost.</td><td>They have different denominators. For revenue $120 and cost $90, margin = 25%, markup ≈ 33.33%.</td></tr><tr><td>Target comparison</td><td>Actual resolution time ≤ target means Met in this practice.</td><td>Some KPIs reward larger values, others smaller values. Read the rule, not the colour alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "tools", "title": "9. Practical spreadsheet tools across Modules 1–5", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Tool / concept</th><th>How to use it</th><th>Purpose and final check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Workbook / worksheet / record / field</td><td>Workbook = file; worksheet = tab; record = row; field = column; cell = row/column intersection.</td><td>One record should remain intact during sorting. Each column should represent one consistently defined field.</td></tr><tr><td>Formatting and freeze panes</td><td>Make headings readable; display dates/numbers appropriately; fit widths; freeze headings for long lists.</td><td>Presentation does not repair wrong types or missing data. Avoid #### and truncated labels.</td></tr><tr><td>Sort</td><td>Select the complete table; choose column and ascending/descending order. For multi-level sorting, set the first grouping then the within-group ordering.</td><td>Sorting only one column can detach values from the correct IDs. A sort changes order; it does not remove records.</td></tr><tr><td>Filter</td><td>Enable header filters; apply every requested condition; inspect matched records and count.</td><td>Filtering temporarily hides non-matches. Keep required filters until related answers are recorded; clear all before questions needing all records.</td></tr><tr><td>Find/replace and cleaning</td><td>Check source, then standardise formats/labels; investigate duplicates, missing data and impossible values.</td><td>Do not turn every missing value into zero or delete an unusual but real event. Keep source context and document important corrections.</td></tr><tr><td>Remove duplicates</td><td>Identify what makes a duplicate, often a repeated unique ID, and compare all relevant fields.</td><td>Repeated category labels are not duplicate records. Preserve the original data if possible.</td></tr><tr><td>Validation</td><td>Data → Data Validation: choose whole number/decimal/date/list rules appropriate to the field.</td><td>A 0–100 score rule prevents many impossible values. It does not prove that an in-range score was entered correctly.</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional formatting</td><td>Home → Conditional Formatting: set value rules, data bars, colour scales or a formula rule.</td><td>Highlights values; does not change them. For whole-row highlighting on A2:H13, <code>=$E2&gt;$F2</code> fixes columns while allowing each row to change.</td></tr><tr><td>PivotTable</td><td>Select the source including headers → Insert → PivotTable. Rows = group; Values = measure; optional Columns and Filters.</td><td>For region means: Rows = Region; Values = ResolutionMinutes set to Average. For counts: count a complete ID field. Check aggregation and source coverage, then refresh after changes.</td></tr><tr><td>Pivot values</td><td>Choose Sum, Count or Average to match the question.</td><td>A Count of numeric times counts non-empty values, not a sum of minutes. Several Values fields summarise separately; an average across two assignment fields is not created just by adding both.</td></tr><tr><td>PivotChart</td><td>Create a chart from the pivot summary.</td><td>It follows that pivot's groups/filters. Check labels, units and refresh state.</td></tr><tr><td>Slicer</td><td>Insert a slicer for a category; connect it to compatible pivots using Report Connections where available.</td><td>Test that every intended chart/pivot responds. An unconnected chart can show a different population.</td></tr><tr><td>Linked sheets</td><td>Use a cross-sheet reference or lookup into another sheet.</td><td>Reduces manual copying, but a bad source link propagates errors. Verify the source and updates.</td></tr><tr><td>Forms and reports</td><td>A form supports structured entry using labelled fields and validation. A report presents selected findings for an audience.</td><td>An attractive form does not validate meaning by itself. A report should identify units, period and relevant totals.</td></tr><tr><td>What-if modelling</td><td>Change a labelled input; let linked formulas recalculate; compare original and changed outcomes.</td><td>Keep other assumptions unchanged if testing one input's effect. This is conditional modelling, not proof of what will happen.</td></tr><tr><td>Goal Seek</td><td>Set cell = the output formula; To value = desired output; By changing cell = one input.</td><td>The output must depend on the input. Check that the solution is feasible. Native menu/tool availability depends on the authorised Excel version.</td></tr><tr><td>Scenario Manager</td><td>Save different input sets, show each and compare the corresponding outputs.</td><td>Know the concept from Module 4. The notification does not guarantee this native tool will be an assessed operation.</td></tr><tr><td>Dashboard evaluation</td><td>Check accuracy, clarity, usability, integrity and targeted improvements.</td><td>Verify calculations, all filter states, source/refresh date, readable units, fair scales and whether it answers the intended user's question.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "charts", "title": "10. Choose, read and evaluate a visualisation", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Representation</th><th>Use when</th><th>Important limitation / check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Table</td><td>Exact values, individual records, several precise comparisons.</td><td>Harder to see a large-scale pattern. Use useful headings, units and sensible precision.</td></tr><tr><td>Bar / column chart</td><td>Compare values across categories, such as regions or products.</td><td>Bars encode length, so use a zero baseline for fair comparisons. Avoid 3D distortion and misleading category widths.</td></tr><tr><td>Line chart</td><td>Show change over time or another meaningful ordered sequence.</td><td>Keep the sequence ordered; label period and scale. Do not imply continuity between unrelated categories.</td></tr><tr><td>Pie / doughnut</td><td>Show a few mutually exclusive parts of one whole.</td><td>Use the same total, sum to the whole and avoid overlaps/too many similar slices. A bar chart often makes close comparisons easier.</td></tr><tr><td>Scatter plot</td><td>Explore the relationship between two quantitative variables.</td><td>Association does not establish causation. Check outliers, group differences and other possible explanations.</td></tr><tr><td>Histogram</td><td>Show the distribution of a numeric variable using intervals.</td><td>Bin boundaries/widths influence appearance. Distinguish intervals from unrelated bar-chart categories.</td></tr><tr><td>Map</td><td>Compare geographic patterns relevant to the decision.</td><td>Raw totals can reflect population size. Use an appropriate denominator and clear legend where relevant.</td></tr><tr><td>Network diagram</td><td>Show connections among entities.</td><td>A connection must have a defined meaning. Clutter can obscure the pattern.</td></tr><tr><td>Infographic / report</td><td>Communicate a selected explanation or findings to an audience.</td><td>Simplification must retain evidence, source, context and relevant limitations.</td></tr><tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Monitor several linked KPIs and explore filters in one view.</td><td>An attractive screen can still contain stale calculations or incompatible filter states.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Read before interpreting:</strong> title → audience/purpose → source → time period → population/filter state → axes and units → both compared values → direction/gap → anomaly/limitation. Check whether a value is a total, mean, rate or percentage.</p><p><strong>Describe only what the evidence supports:</strong> a single snapshot supports a comparison, not a time trend. Two endpoints show net change, not necessarily a steady rise. A correlation, rating difference or high workload alone does not prove a cause.</p><p><strong>Watch for misleading design:</strong> truncated bar axes, unequal intervals, cherry-picked dates, excluded groups, missing denominators, 3D area/volume exaggeration, ambiguous labels, unlabelled dual axes and selective colour emphasis.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Good dashboard / UX feature</th><th>Why it helps</th><th>Observable evaluation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Relevant KPIs near the top</td><td>The manager can identify what needs attention.</td><td>Example criterion: at least 4 of 5 representative users identify the worst-performing region correctly within 10 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Readable labels, units and period</td><td>Users compare the right quantities.</td><td>Ask a user to explain one chart accurately without extra instructions.</td></tr><tr><td>Consistent colours with text/icons</td><td>Meanings remain understandable without relying on colour alone.</td><td>Check status recognition in greyscale and with representative users.</td></tr><tr><td>Visible filters and a reset option</td><td>Users know which population they are seeing.</td><td>Apply/reset a filter and verify all intended outputs update.</td></tr><tr><td>Source and last-refresh timestamp</td><td>Users can judge currency and detect stale data.</td><td>Compare against a known source update and verify the displayed refresh state.</td></tr><tr><td>Keyboard access and usable mobile layout</td><td>More users can complete the required tasks.</td><td>Test focus order, controls, text size and layout on the intended devices.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "quality", "title": "11. Data, information, quality and limitations", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Enterprise example / consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Data → information → decision</td><td>Raw observations become useful information through processing and interpretation.</td><td>Ticket records → regional means/rates → a monitored trial to reduce delays. A numeric calculation alone is not the recommendation.</td></tr><tr><td>Quantitative / categorical</td><td>Quantitative = amounts/counts; categorical = groups or labels.</td><td>Ticket counts are discrete; time is a measured quantity. Region is nominal; Low/Medium/High urgency is ordinal.</td></tr><tr><td>Primary / secondary data</td><td>Primary is collected first-hand for a purpose; secondary was collected by someone else or for another purpose.</td><td>Check relevance, collection method, definitions and permissions. Neither type is automatically accurate.</td></tr><tr><td>Structured / unstructured</td><td>Structured follows a defined schema; unstructured has no fixed tabular record structure.</td><td>Ticket fields are structured; free-text comments/images need interpretation or coding before many summaries.</td></tr><tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>Values correctly represent the real thing.</td><td>A mistyped 60-minute record distorts a mean. Verify against the source.</td></tr><tr><td>Reliability</td><td>A source/method yields dependable, consistent measurements.</td><td>An inconsistent timer or unstable feed weakens comparisons.</td></tr><tr><td>Validity</td><td>Values and methods fit the rules and intended measurement.</td><td>A score can pass a 0–100 validation rule and still be factually wrong.</td></tr><tr><td>Completeness</td><td>Required records and fields are present.</td><td>Missing urgent tickets may understate delay rates. Blank is not necessarily zero.</td></tr><tr><td>Timeliness / relevance / consistency</td><td>Data is current, answers this question and uses comparable definitions/units.</td><td>Comparing hours with minutes or last year's demand with this week's staffing can mislead.</td></tr><tr><td>Error</td><td>A mistake in collection, entry, processing or presentation.</td><td>Wrong reference, duplicate row or faulty sensor. Identify what measure it changes.</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty</td><td>Incomplete knowledge or precision, including sampling variation.</td><td>A small sample gives an unstable estimate. Explain the uncertainty without inventing a statistical confidence level.</td></tr><tr><td>Bias</td><td>A systematic distortion or exclusion.</td><td>Surveying only weekday visitors may omit weekend users. A larger dataset can repeat the same bias.</td></tr><tr><td>Limitation</td><td>A restriction on what the evidence can answer.</td><td>Revenue without costs cannot establish profit; a dashboard without case mix cannot establish staff efficiency.</td></tr><tr><td>Outlier</td><td>A markedly unusual observation.</td><td>Investigate whether it is an error or a real event. Do not remove it merely to improve the average.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Specific issue</th><th>How it could affect the decision</th><th>Targeted improvement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Optional satisfaction survey</td><td>Respondents may differ from non-respondents; the direction of bias is not known merely because a survey is optional.</td><td>Report response count/rate; include relevant groups and use a more systematic collection method.</td></tr><tr><td>Missing category/urgency</td><td>Longer times may reflect harder tickets, so blaming staff or adding staff may be inappropriate.</td><td>Compare similar case types and gather staffing, workload and cost information.</td></tr><tr><td>Unequal regional ticket totals</td><td>The region with more failures may still have a lower failure rate.</td><td>Calculate failures ÷ tickets for each region and report both counts and rates.</td></tr><tr><td>Short/seasonal time window</td><td>A temporary event may be mistaken for a stable pattern.</td><td>Use comparable periods and investigate seasonality or disruptions.</td></tr><tr><td>Missing costs</td><td>The highest revenue option may have a lower profit.</td><td>Collect relevant costs before claiming profitability.</td></tr><tr><td>Inconsistent units or missing records</td><td>Means and rates may be distorted or incomparable.</td><td>Standardise units, check source coverage and explain unresolved omissions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "lessons", "title": "12. Booklet Lessons 14–19: concise theory table", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Lesson</th><th>Know this</th><th>How to apply it in an answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>14 · UX in visualisation, Part 2</td><td>UX concerns whether users can complete tasks effectively and understand the result. Consider accessibility, layout, interactivity, device suitability and measurable criteria. AI/AR/VR should solve a user need.</td><td>Identify the specific user/task → propose a feature → explain its benefit → give a practical evaluation method. A novel feature is not automatically useful.</td></tr><tr><td>15 · Creating visualisations</td><td>Research relevant sources; check/organise/store data; choose a visual for the purpose; identify trends, patterns and relationships; qualify predictions.</td><td>Explain how preparation and chart choice reveal the needed evidence. Forecasts depend on data/assumptions and may fail when conditions change.</td></tr><tr><td>16 · Data security</td><td>Authentication checks identity; authorisation limits actions; encryption protects readability; backups support recovery. Audit trails help trace access/changes.</td><td>Match a control to a risk. For example: restrict editing of a staff dashboard to authorised managers and log changes to reduce unauthorised alteration.</td></tr><tr><td>17 · Presenting data</td><td>Choose graphs, tables, maps, networks, dashboards, infographics or reports for the audience and task. Comments/likes are alternative feedback, not necessarily representative measures.</td><td>A manager may need a KPI summary and drill-down; an analyst may need exact records and methods. Keep the evidence consistent for both audiences.</td></tr><tr><td>18 · Errors, bias and uncertainty</td><td>Distinguish raw records from processed summaries; distinguish errors, uncertainty, bias and limitations; check collection, analysis and reporting.</td><td>Name the issue → explain its effect on a measure → connect it to the recommendation → propose specific additional evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>19 · Blockchain</td><td>A distributed ledger stores records in linked blocks; hash links and agreement mechanisms help make alteration evident and support shared verification.</td><td>A supply chain can share a traceable history, but false physical-world inputs can still be recorded. Consider access/privacy, cost, governance and whether a simpler shared database meets the need.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "enterprise", "title": "13. Enterprises, security, ethics and innovative systems", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Need / technology</th><th>Mechanism and potential benefit</th><th>Risk / response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Live dashboard</td><td>A data feed updates shared KPIs so managers can identify queues or shortages earlier.</td><td>Stale or incorrect feeds can spread wrong information. Show refresh status, validate inputs and keep a fallback process.</td></tr><tr><td>Automated ticket routing</td><td>Rules or a model assign tickets to suitable queues/staff, reducing manual sorting.</td><td>Misclassification can delay urgent work. Use human override, review exceptions and measure routing accuracy/delay.</td></tr><tr><td>AI prediction</td><td>A model learns patterns in historical data to estimate demand and guide resource planning.</td><td>Biased or changing data can produce poor estimates. Test on relevant unseen data, monitor errors by group and retain review.</td></tr><tr><td>Database / shared system</td><td>Consistent records and permissions reduce scattered copies and allow coordinated updates.</td><td>Bad definitions or permissions can still damage quality/privacy. Validate, govern access, back up and train users.</td></tr><tr><td>Blockchain</td><td>Participants share a tamper-evident record for agreed tracking/verification.</td><td>It does not verify the physical truth of an input or remove the need for governance. Assess suitability and input checks.</td></tr><tr><td>MFA and access control</td><td>Different authentication factors help verify identity; role-based permissions restrict viewing/editing.</td><td>Authentication alone does not limit what a valid account can access. Assign the minimum necessary rights and review them.</td></tr><tr><td>Encryption and backups</td><td>Encryption protects data without the key; tested backups allow recovery after loss or corruption.</td><td>Encryption is not backup. Protect keys and check that restoration actually works.</td></tr><tr><td>Privacy and responsible use</td><td>Use personal information for an appropriate, explained purpose, minimise unnecessary collection and control access/sharing.</td><td>An exported dashboard can expose individual details. Aggregate/anonymise where appropriate and restrict exports.</td></tr><tr><td>Intellectual property and Indigenous data</td><td>Respect source rights, licences, attribution and relevant cultural/community authority and protocols.</td><td>Having access to data does not grant unrestricted reuse. Check agreed purposes, access and permissions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Fit the system to the enterprise:</strong> explain the need, users, scale, cost/resources and required outcome. A small shop may need a simple stock spreadsheet; a multi-site service enterprise may need shared records and timely dashboards. Justify the fit rather than assuming the newest technology is best.</p><p><strong>Evaluate a proposed change:</strong> name the new workflow, its operational effect, a contextual risk, the transition/support needed, and a measure of success. A monitored trial can compare delay rate, error rate, staff time, cost and user success against a baseline.</p>"}, {"id": "responses", "title": "14. Written response structures and a worked dashboard", "html": "<p>This separate fictional four-week dashboard covers a different population from the 12-ticket CSV. Never mix its figures with the sample's means.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Tickets</th><th>Over target</th><th>Mean resolution</th><th>Survey responses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>North</td><td>200</td><td>60</td><td>32 min</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>South</td><td>320</td><td>64</td><td>27.5 min</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>West</td><td>160</td><td>24</td><td>24 min</td><td>30</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Command / question</th><th>Answer structure</th><th>Worked example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identify / state</td><td>Name exactly what is asked; add the unit where relevant.</td><td>North has a 30% over-target rate.</td></tr><tr><td>Compare</td><td>Both figures → direction → difference → units/context.</td><td>North: 60/200 = 30%; South: 64/320 = 20%. North is 10 percentage points higher, despite fewer over-target tickets in total.</td></tr><tr><td>Describe a pattern</td><td>What changes/stands out → specific evidence → scope.</td><td>In this snapshot, North has the longest mean resolution time: 32 minutes versus South's 27.5 and West's 24. A snapshot does not prove a time trend.</td></tr><tr><td>Explain</td><td>Cause/mechanism → how/why → consequence.</td><td>Raw counts can mislead because South processes more tickets; dividing by each region's total makes the rates comparable.</td></tr><tr><td>Recommend / justify</td><td>Action → figures → why action fits → enterprise benefit.</td><td>Trial improved triage support in North. It has a 30% over-target rate versus South's 20%, and a 32-minute mean versus 27.5. A monitored trial could reduce delays and improve service reliability.</td></tr><tr><td>Explain a limitation</td><td>Specific issue → effect on evidence → effect on decision → evidence needed.</td><td>Case complexity is missing. North may have harder cases, so its longer times do not establish poorer staff performance. Compare category/urgency groups and collect workload/costs before making a permanent staffing decision.</td></tr><tr><td>Analyse a system change</td><td>Changed workflow → linked benefit → contextual risk → response.</td><td>Automatic routing could assign tickets to relevant staff sooner. Incorrect categories could misroute urgent jobs. Keep human override, train users and monitor routing errors and delay rates during a trial.</td></tr><tr><td>Evaluate / assess</td><td>Weigh relevant benefits and limitations → justified judgement.</td><td>A live dashboard is suitable if timely, accurate data supports the managers' decisions and the benefit justifies the cost. Pilot it with refresh monitoring and compare delay/error rates before broader rollout.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Use evidence honestly:</strong> recommend a feasible action, not a claim that the dashboard proves its cause. Avoid 'the data is biased' without a mechanism. Avoid 'improves efficiency' without explaining which work changes and how.</p><p><strong>Five assessment criteria translated:</strong> meet enterprise needs = fit solution to problem/users; data and information = explain records → useful summaries; analytical tools = choose and apply correctly; innovation = analyse workflow effects; communication = write clearly for the specified audience, with relevant evidence and units.</p>"}, {"id": "errors", "title": "15. Formula errors and misleading results", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely explanation</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>#N/A</code></td><td>Exact lookup has no match, or numeric data has no mode.</td><td>ID spelling, spaces, text/number type, missing IDs, intended match mode. For MODE with no repeats, report no mode.</td></tr><tr><td><code>#REF!</code></td><td>Invalid/deleted reference or a return position outside the selected range.</td><td>Check references and lookup column count. A table A:G has seven columns.</td></tr><tr><td><code>#DIV/0!</code></td><td>Division by zero, no numeric data to average, or no matching numeric values in a conditional mean.</td><td>Inspect the denominator and whether the intended records exist. Do not replace unknown results with zero.</td></tr><tr><td><code>#VALUE!</code></td><td>An argument has the wrong type or structure.</td><td>Check text such as 'twenty', mismatched multi-condition ranges and accidental spaces/units in numeric fields.</td></tr><tr><td><code>#NAME?</code></td><td>Excel does not recognise a function/name.</td><td>Check function spelling and quoted text. Use straight quotes.</td></tr><tr><td><code>#NUM!</code></td><td>A numeric argument/calculation is outside what the function can handle.</td><td>Review the numerical inputs and function requirements.</td></tr><tr><td><code>####</code></td><td>Display is too narrow, or certain date/time values cannot be displayed in that format.</td><td>Widen the column and check the format/value. This is not necessarily a calculation error.</td></tr><tr><td>Formula appears as text</td><td>Cell is text-formatted, entry has a leading apostrophe, or Show Formulas is on.</td><td>Check view and cell format, then re-enter the formula if necessary.</td></tr><tr><td>Plausible but wrong result</td><td>Wrong scope, range, boundary, copied reference, summary operation or unit.</td><td>Check a known row manually, first/last references, active filters and source definitions. No error message does not prove accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>Result does not update</td><td>Formula/linked object may not reflect the changed input, or a pivot may need refresh.</td><td>Check dependencies, calculation/refresh state, source coverage and whether a chart uses the intended summary.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "checklist", "title": "16. Final practice and test checklist", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Do this</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Before practising</td><td>Know your field meanings, units, record grain, reporting period and targets. Work from a fresh copy and use the approved Excel environment.</td></tr><tr><td>At each question</td><td>Underline the task: list records, total, count, mean, lookup, rule, compare or recommend. Select the tool that answers that task.</td></tr><tr><td>For filters</td><td>Apply every condition; record IDs/count while the correct filters are active; clear filters when required for the next task.</td></tr><tr><td>For formulas</td><td>Use correct/complete ranges; align labels and values; use exact IDs; quote text correctly; lock only fixed references; test equality boundaries.</td></tr><tr><td>For a changed input</td><td>Record the original formula and result first. Change the requested value, inspect the output and restore it if the question says to.</td></tr><tr><td>For results</td><td>Include the requested formula, numerical value, unit and precision. Check one calculation independently and ask whether the magnitude makes sense.</td></tr><tr><td>For dashboard answers</td><td>Check population/period and denominators. Quote both comparison figures. Connect recommendations to evidence and acknowledge a specific limitation.</td></tr><tr><td>For submission</td><td>Record responses in Canvas as you go and submit before time ends. Your school practice warns workbook changes can be temporary; avoid losing them by refreshing.</td></tr><tr><td>Suggested rehearsal timing</td><td>4 minutes reading; 42 spreadsheet; 28 written; 6 checking. This is a practice allocation, not an official section time limit.</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum readiness check</td><td>Without notes: apply two filters; write AVERAGEIF, exact VLOOKUP and IF on changed data; explain a percentage-point gap; justify an action, limitation and system change.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "sources", "title": "Sources and boundaries", "html": "<p>Based on the supplied assessment notification; teacher preparation guidance; Data Skills with Spreadsheets Modules 1–5 (Lessons 1–16); Week 4 Dispatch Performance and IT Support Ticket practice (pages 49–53); and Booklet 2 Lessons 14–19. The worked ticket data and dashboard are original fictional practice. This is a revision reference, not an official formula list or a forecast of exact questions.</p><p>Primary Excel references used to check function details: <a href=\"https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/functions/vlookup-function\">VLOOKUP</a>, <a href=\"https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/functions/averageif-function\">AVERAGEIF</a>, <a href=\"https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/functions/subtotal-function\">SUBTOTAL</a>, <a href=\"https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/functions/mode-sngl-function\">MODE.SNGL</a>, <a href=\"https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/functions/stdev-s-function\">STDEV.S</a>.</p><p>Function availability, menu locations and window-switching methods can differ by Excel version and lockdown configuration. Learn the concepts and practise the specific environment your teacher authorises.</p>"}];
